  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{3})}^{x}+1?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{3})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/ff62aa6e655becc0a34af9f47b89d06ea8b22fec1644f8a4b1edacb82b794e83.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{3})}^{x}+1,$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{3})}^{x}$ and shift it $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/f66b03cccba11fccc8574ba757cacf3fa87f226644f1c422a34a3c56908819ad.png)",
